.TH SUBC 1 "2026-08-31" "SUB Language" "SUB Language Manual"
.SH NAME
subc \- compile a SUB program to a native executable
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B subc
.I input.sb
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B subc
compiles a SUB source file to a native executable. On x86-64 Linux it
encodes the machine code and writes the ELF itself, so no C compiler,
assembler or linker is involved and the result is a static binary.
.PP
Programs that use a construct the machine-code backend does not implement
yet are built through a C compiler instead, and
.B subc
names the construct that forced the fallback.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-o " file"
Write the executable to
.IR file .
The default is the source name without its extension.
.TP
.B \-\-native
Emit machine code directly. Fail rather than fall back to a C compiler.
.TP
.B \-\-via\-c
Generate C and build it with
.BR $CC ,
defaulting to
.BR gcc .
.TP
.BR \-O0 ", " \-O1 ", " \-O2 ", " \-O3
Passed through to the C compiler. Only meaningful with
.BR \-\-via\-c .
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Report which backend ran and why.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CC
The C compiler used by
.BR \-\-via\-c .
.TP
.B SUB_PATH
Searched by
.B import
before the bundled standard library.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
An executable was produced.
.TP
.B 1
Compilation failed, or
.B \-\-native
was given for a program the machine-code backend cannot build.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
.nf
subc hello.sb
subc hello.sb --native -o hello
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sub (1),
.BR subi (1)
.SH BUGS
Report at https://github.com/subhobhai943/sub-lang/issues
